# Compose's own interpolation file. Copy to ./.env and fill it in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT THE SERVER'S CONFIGURATION. Two files, and confusing them
# costs an afternoon:
#
#   ./.env       read by COMPOSE, for ${...} substitution in docker-compose.yml
#   ./lawha.env  read by LAWHA-SERVER, inside the container
#
# An `env_file:` entry reaches the container and is invisible to compose; a
# ./.env value is expanded by compose and never reaches the server. They are
# not interchangeable, and a setting written in the wrong one is silently
# ignored rather than rejected.
#
# Everything here has a working default, so an empty ./.env still comes up.

# --- the published port -----------------------------------------------------
#
# The one port this stack binds on the host. The gateway maps a name like
# http://lawha.local onto it, so this number has to match what you tell the
# gateway when you add the link.
#
# PICK FROM 9001–9099.
#
# NEVER 80. That port belongs to the gateway, and taking it does not fail
# loudly — it takes every other project's name off the network at the same
# time, which presents as "the network is broken" rather than as anything to
# do with Lawha.
#
# NEVER 32768 OR ABOVE. Linux draws its ephemeral outbound ports from that
# range, so a service parked there binds fine most days and occasionally loses
# the race to a random outbound connection. Intermittent, and it looks like the
# app crashing rather than like a port collision.
LAWHA_PUBLISHED_PORT=9002

# --- where the data lives ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Both are directories ON THE HOST, and both must be ABSOLUTE paths. A relative
# one resolves against this file and lands the database back inside the
# repository, where a rename or a fresh clone loses it.
#
# `~` does expand here, in short form, long form and through an interpolated
# default. Leave these commented to accept the defaults below.
#
# LAWHA_DATA_DIR=~/lawha-data          # the database and uploaded images
# LAWHA_BACKUP_DIR=~/lawha-backups     # the backup archive

# --- HTTPS in the stack (optional) -------------------------------------------
#
# Off by default, and off is a supported deployment rather than an oversight:
# this stack is designed to sit behind a gateway that owns port 80 and speaks
# for the network (ADR 0018). If yours terminates TLS, leave this alone.
#
# Turn it on when nothing in front of you does TLS and the traffic crosses a
# network you do not own. What plain HTTP actually costs, stated plainly: the
# session cookie travels in the clear on every request, so anyone who can watch
# the wire — another machine on the same Wi-Fi, a guest on the office network —
# can copy it and become you. There is no password to crack and nothing in any
# log afterwards.
#
# TWO STEPS, and the second one is the one people skip:
#
#   ./run.sh tls        mints a local CA and a leaf for <stack>.local
#   LAWHA_TLS=on        here, then ./run.sh
#
# ...and then INSTALL certs/lawha-ca.pem on every device. A certificate signed
# by a CA nobody trusts produces a warning on every visit, and a warning people
# are trained to dismiss is worse than no TLS at all — it trains them past the
# warning everywhere else too.
#
# The plain port above keeps working. Both listeners serve the same app.
#
#LAWHA_TLS=on
#LAWHA_TLS_PORT=9443

# --- more than one Lawha on this machine (optional) --------------------------
#
# Leave this unset for the ordinary case — one Lawha on the box. Unset means the
# containers keep the names they have always had (`lawha-server`, `lawha-app`,
# `lawha-backup`, `lawha-ngrok`) and every command in the documentation works
# unchanged.
#
# Set it, and this stack gets its own container names and its own compose
# project. That is what a second deployment needs: a container name is unique
# per docker DAEMON, not per project, so four fixed names used to mean exactly
# one Lawha per machine.
#
# THE THREE SETTINGS IN THIS BLOCK ARE ONE SETTING. LAWHA_STACK on its own gives
# you two deployments bind-mounting one ~/lawha-data — two servers, two WAL
# connections, one lawha.db. SQLite does not refuse that and neither does
# docker; it presents as boards disappearing from whichever dashboard you are
# not looking at. `./run.sh` refuses to start rather than let it happen, which
# is why LAWHA_DATA_DIR and LAWHA_BACKUP_DIR stop being optional the moment
# this line is uncommented.
#
# A second stack also needs its own LAWHA_PUBLISHED_PORT above (still 9001–9099)
# and, if you use the gateway, its own route: `portless alias lawha2 9003`. The
# route name follows LAWHA_STACK.
#
#LAWHA_STACK=lawha2
#LAWHA_DATA_DIR=~/lawha-data-lawha2
#LAWHA_BACKUP_DIR=~/lawha-backups-lawha2

# --- reaching it from outside the network (optional) -------------------------
#
# Leave both blank and nothing changes: the ngrok service sits behind a compose
# profile and does not start with `docker compose up -d`. It starts only with
# `./run.sh public`, or `docker compose --profile public up -d`.
#
# Fill them in and that command puts Lawha on the public internet at a fixed
# https address, while http://lawha.local keeps working on the LAN.
#
# BEFORE YOU DO: registration is open on this deployment
# (LAWHA_ALLOW_OPEN_REGISTRATION=true in lawha.env), so anyone who has the link
# can create an account. That is a reasonable default on a private network and
# a decision worth making again before the address is public.

# Your account's authtoken, from
# https://dashboard.ngrok.com/get-started/your-authtoken
#
# This is your whole ngrok account. Treat it the way you treat
# LAWHA_MASTER_PASSWORD — this file is gitignored for both of them.
#NGROK_AUTHTOKEN=

# The domain permanently assigned to your ngrok account, from
# https://dashboard.ngrok.com/domains — it looks like
# `something-random.ngrok-free.dev`.
#
# WITH the scheme, e.g. https://abc123.ngrok-free.dev
#
# Every free account gets one and it does not expire, which is what makes the
# link you hand out survive a restart. Leave it blank and ngrok mints a fresh
# random hostname on every start, so the address you gave people last week is
# gone — which is why `run.sh public` refuses to start without it.
#
# Free-plan ceilings worth knowing before you rely on this: 1 GB of transfer
# and 20,000 HTTP requests a month, and first-time visitors get an ngrok
# interstitial page to click through. A collaborative canvas is not a light
# user of either.
#NGROK_DOMAIN=
